283
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:52,750
So I'll just-- let's start
with the else right here.

284
00:11:52,750 --> 00:11:56,190
If my search key is not
less than or not greater

285
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:58,410
than the root key, then
I know I've found it.

286
00:11:58,410 --> 00:11:59,760
And I return my string here.

287
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:01,170
I found the key.

288
00:12:01,170 --> 00:12:03,768
And really, this could
just be a true or false.

289
00:12:03,768 --> 00:12:05,560
But here, it just
prints, it found the key.

290
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:08,893
So effectively, the same thing.

291
00:12:08,893 --> 00:12:10,560
But then what about
these if statements?

292
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:11,630
Well, if the search
key I'm looking for

293
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:14,088
is less than the root, that
means I need to go to the left.

294
00:12:14,088 --> 00:12:16,020
So look at my
recursive call here.

295
00:12:16,020 --> 00:12:18,510
Go to the left and add
the search key right here.

296
00:12:18,510 --> 00:12:20,330
So now, I go to the
left with a search key

297
00:12:20,330 --> 00:12:21,690
and do this logic again.

298
00:12:21,690 --> 00:12:23,300
Well, if the search key is
greater than the root key,

299
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:25,467
then I do the same thing,
but I make a function call

300
00:12:25,467 --> 00:12:26,910
to the right.

301
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:27,730
And that's it.

302
00:12:27,730 --> 00:12:30,150
And let me point out one
little difference between this

303
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:33,340
and the previous root here.

304
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:34,770
Notice the name root.

305
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:36,820
But this is going
to change each time.

306
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:39,870
So root node might have
been a little bit better

307
00:12:39,870 --> 00:12:42,090
of a variable name,
although calling it

308
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:45,720
a root node, even when you're
