324
00:13:27,370 --> 00:13:28,730
the structure of the tree.

325
00:13:28,730 --> 00:13:32,200
And it does that by finding
the minimum in the right.

326
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:34,640
Here, it uses the find minimum.

327
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:39,760
It goes and says, give me the
find min of my right subtree,

328
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:42,740
and that's going to be the one
I replace this one with here.

329
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:45,280
But as you may
recall, you could also

330
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:49,370
use the find max
in my left subtree.

331
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:51,670
They would both effectively
do the same thing.

332
00:13:51,670 --> 00:13:54,340
And that's really it, all right.

333
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:57,340
That is find, inserts, and
deletes in a binary search tree.

334
00:13:57,340 --> 00:14:00,822
Let me just run it really
quick, and we'll take a look.

335
00:14:00,822 --> 00:14:04,940
And then lastly, we're going to
talk about traversing a tree,

336
00:14:04,940 --> 00:14:05,990
OK.

337
00:14:05,990 --> 00:14:07,890
So here, you can take a look.

338
00:14:07,890 --> 00:14:09,942
Here's our tree down here.

339
00:14:09,942 --> 00:14:12,650
I should also mention, if you're
looking at the code in my GitHub

